@startuml
title SeparatorWidthEnforcer - guarantee a required separator is >= 3 cells wide at EVERY row/column, not just in aggregate

start
:Scan every cell in the region\n(after SeamWidener has already run);

if (biome's bounded run on this axis\n(contiguous same-biome cells, capped\non BOTH ends by a real, different\nbiome - not map edge/unknown) is\n< 3 cells, AND the two capping\nbiomes are mutually incompatible\nwith EACH OTHER (this run is\nactually bridging them)?) then (no)
  :Not a required-separator thin spot -\nskip (ordinary blob tapering is left\nalone on purpose, see scope note);
else (yes)
  while (run still < 3 wide,\nsome side still stealable?) is (yes)
    if (stealing the cell just past the\nLEFT boundary legal here (against ALL\nits neighbors) AND safe (donor stays\nconnected, stays at/above its own\nminimum size)?) then (no)
      if (same check on the RIGHT\nboundary instead?) then (no)
        :Neither side stealable right now -\nstop, leave the remaining width\nas-is (never force);
      else (yes)
        :Steal the right-boundary cell;
      endif
    else (yes)
      :Steal the left-boundary cell;
    endif
  endwhile (reached width 3,\nor gave up)
endif

stop

note right
  Scope deliberately narrower than "every patch >= 3
  wide everywhere": ordinary round blobs naturally taper
  to 1-2 cells wide at their own tips, and enforcing 3
  there would fight normal organic shape, not fix a
  defect. This pass only targets cells acting as a
  REQUIRED separator - bridging two biomes that have no
  AdjacencyRule with each other, the exact "1-cell token
  seam standing in for a real buffer" DESIGN.md forbids
  (same framing PatchSizeEnforcer/SeparatorWidener
  already use for a separator's TOTAL size).

  Confirmed on real data (seed 42): SeamWidener's own
  fix already ran and hit its aggregate cell-count
  target (MIN_SEAM_WIDTH_CELLS=9) for the whole pinch
  cluster, but a whole-cluster total doesn't guarantee
  every individual row/column meets it - a real plains
  separator between savanna/ocean (mutually
  incompatible) stayed 3-wide along most of its run but
  narrowed to exactly 2 at several specific rows even
  after SeamWidener. This pass targets those exact
  rows/columns directly instead of an aggregate.

  Also covers width-1 separator pinches PinchResolver
  structurally cannot fix: giving a 1-wide separator cell
  away to either flanking biome would put the two
  incompatible flanks directly next to each other -
  GrowthEngine.isLegal already blocks that candidate, so
  PinchResolver correctly leaves it alone. Widening (this
  pass) is the only valid fix for that shape.

  Same safety primitives reused throughout: GrowthEngine
  .isLegal, SeparatorWidener.stealingPreservesDonor
  Connectivity, SeparatorOrganicizer.donorComponentSize
  Without/minCellsFor. Looped to a fixed point, MAX_PASSES
  cap, never forces past what's legally/safely stealable.
end note
@enduml
